    "desc": "Kalamkari means pen-work: at Srikalahasti artists free-hand entire Ramayana and temple epics onto cotton with a bamboo pen charged with fermented black iron-jaggery solution, while Machilipatnam workshops print Persian-inspired florals with carved blocks for export. The cloth is scoured in myrobalan and cow dung, mordanted with alum, and dipped repeatedly so reds come from madder or chay root, blacks from iron, blues from indigo. Seventeen-plus wash-and-dry stages mean the palette is earned, not applied, which is why old kalamkari smells faintly of fermented fruit and carries a soft, tea-toned ground. Its export reach once stretched to Safavid Iran and Javanese courts, feeding the wider Indian-cotton trade.",
