    "sum": "Gond and Pardhan-Gond painting fills animals, trees and deities with rhythmic dotwork, danglers and pattern-fields invented as a modern school by Jangarh Singh Shyam.",
    "desc": "What the market calls Gond art began when Jangarh Singh Shyam, a Pardhan storyteller, translated his community's dhigna wall decorations onto paper and canvas in the early 1980s, guided by Bharat Bhavan in Bhopal. Forms are built from flowing contour lines that are then textured with signature fills — fine dots (dhek), S-curves, fish-scale fans, tadpole danglers — so a deer or a bara dev tree shimmers like woven basketry. Original wall versions marked walls of Gond homes at festivals, while the modern school spread through entire families in Patangarh into auction houses and the Bhopal Biennale circuit. Acrylic-on-canvas colors are far brighter than the mud-and-lime originals, but the cadence of repeated marks stays the tradition's fingerprint.",
