    "desc": "Rajput painting served courts that kept Hindu and folk allegiances while Mughal technique circulated: artists on paper and palm-leaf used flat fields of red, yellow, green and the electric white of ground shell, profile figures with lotus eyes, and gold-and-silver work such as Basohli's famous beetle-wing shimmer. Favorite subjects were the Bhagavata Purana's Krishna cycle, Gita Govinda, Ragamala music-pictures and Baramasa seasonal series, often read as courtly allegory. Regional schools diverge boldly — Mewar's wiry energy, Bundi-Kota's lush forests, Kishangarh's serpentine idealized faces epitomized by Nihal Chand's Bani-Thani, and the Kangra school's misty lyrical washes. Painting traveled as manuscripts, albums and loose leaves, tied into lacquered covers rather than framed.",
