    "desc": "Pichwai (pichh = behind, wai = hanging) is functional liturgy: the textile backdrop sets the season's mood for Shrinathji, the child-form Krishna enshrined at Nathdwara since 1672. Annakoot pichwais pile mountains of food, Sharad Purnima works silver the night lotus pond, and kamal talai lotus fields, cows and mor (peacock) motifs recur like stanzas. Nathdwara workshops standardized a refined style — symmetrical compostions, detailed natural observation, gold and silver accents on deep grounds — while earlier Madhubani-adjacent and Deccani influences survive in older examples. The paintings doubled as portable devotion for pilgrims, which is why pichwai is simultaneously temple furniture, calendar art and collectible painting.",
